In article <11500@helios.TAMU.EDU> sre9018@csseq.tamu.edu (Steven Ray Evatt) writes:
>I am not sure if this is a bug, but a friend of mine
>pointed it out to me and we agree that it really should
>not be this way.
>
>Well, when ever you walk into a room, everything in it
>(monsters and equipment) shows up.  And when you leave
>the room, you can remember where all the equipment is, 
>yet all the monster disappear because you don't know 
>where they are moving to.  
>
>But this allows one to locate mimics in shops, (and elsewhere)
>because when you walk into a shop, everything shows up.
>Even then mimic that you think is a piece of armor, but
>when you leave, the armor disappears, revealing it's 
>true identity as a mimic.
>
>We believe that since up to that point the player still
>thinks that the mimic is a piece of equipment, that once
>you leave the room, that he should not dissappear.

But it's not necessarily as simple as that, Steven.

No "mimicking" ability can produce a -perfect- facsimile, after all. 
Furthermore, the human(oid) subconscious mind is less capable of retaining the 
memory of an imperfect, incongruous, "wrong" stimulus than a _bona fide_ one. 
Hence, the player's memory of the mimicked object quickly fades, while the 
memory of the real objects remains.

I suppose that, in a perfect game, there should be a short time lapse before 
the memory-fade occurs. But it's a lot simpler code-wise and more traditional 
to leave things the way they are now.

